======================================================================================================================
ITEM 29 -- fast bars: R = R_corotation/R_bar in TWO independent Tremaine-Weinberg samples (MaNGA, then CALIFA)
======================================================================================================================
  Geron+2023 table 3: 225 rows, 210 with a finite R and positive published bounds; 113 strong bars, 97 weak
  R: median 1.663, 16-84% [1.038, 2.715], max 11.63; published half-width: median 0.578 (16-84%: 0.267 - 1.637)
  raw: 33.3 +- 3.3 per cent of bars have R < 1.4 (the list's criterion is >= 90 per cent); 11.9 per cent have R < 1, which is dynamically impossible
  per-galaxy verdicts from the published bounds: 79 individually SLOW (lower bound above 1.4), 30 individually FAST (upper bound below 1.4), 101 undecided
     Strong bar : N = 113, median R = 1.535, 42 per cent fast
     Weak bar   : N =  97, median R = 1.881, 24 per cent fast
  R is strongly correlated with its OWN published uncertainty: corr(R, sigma) = +0.643, median sigma/R = 0.354
  error bin       N  median R  frac R<1.4  frac R<1 (impossible)
  0-0.2          20     0.995        0.95                   0.50
  0.2-0.5        72     1.514        0.47                   0.15
  0.5-1.5        81     1.830        0.17                   0.05
  >1.5           37     2.709        0.08                   0.00
  the best-measured 20 bars have a median R of 0.99 and 50 per cent of them are DYNAMICALLY IMPOSSIBLE; the worst-measured 37 have a median R of 2.71.  The two ends of the
  same catalogue give opposite answers, and the end with the smallest error bars is the one that is unphysical.
  maximum likelihood: intrinsic slow fraction f = 0.00 (profile 1-sigma [0.00, 0.01]) with a global error-scale factor s = 1.25; f = 1 is disfavoured by Delta log L = -259
  POSTERIOR PREDICTIVE CHECK of that best fit: it predicts 37.7 +- 3.2 per cent unphysical R < 1 against the observed 11.9 (-8.1 sigma), and a median R of 1.19 +- 0.05 against the observed 1.66 (+9.4 sigma)
  so the Gaussian-error deconvolution does NOT reproduce the data it was fitted to.  Its maximum-likelihood f is an
  artefact of the R-sigma correlation -- objects with large R also carry large error bars, so the fit is free to call
  them badly-measured fast bars, while paying nothing for the unphysical tail it then predicts and the data do not show.
  The ML value is recorded but MUST NOT be quoted as the intrinsic slow fraction.
  INJECTION f_true = 0.0 -> recovered 0.00 +- 0.00 over 10 synthetic samples
  INJECTION f_true = 0.3 -> recovered 0.29 +- 0.02 over 10 synthetic samples
  INJECTION f_true = 0.7 -> recovered 0.70 +- 0.04 over 10 synthetic samples
  [PASS] 29a MUTATION CONTROL (injection/recovery): the deconvolution is unbiased under its own assumptions -- synthetic samples with a known slow fraction, built with the real per-galaxy uncertainties, come back with that fraction.  The estimator is therefore not the thing that is broken, which is what makes its predictive failure above a statement about the published error model   (f_true 0.0 -> 0.00 +- 0.00; f_true 0.3 -> 0.29 +- 0.02; f_true 0.7 -> 0.70 +- 0.04)
  the one systematic that moves every point the same way is the adopted bar length, since R scales as 1/R_bar: for 90 per cent of this sample to be fast, bar radii would have to be UNDERestimated by a factor 2.17; for the median bar, by 1.19.  Conversely the 12 per cent with R < 1 require bar radii OVERestimated for those objects.
  Cuomo et al. (2021, A&A 649, A30) reached the same conclusion for the 'ultrafast' bars in the CALIFA Tremaine-Weinberg
  samples: they are a bar-length measurement problem.  The same systematic is free to move the slow tail the other way.
  
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SECOND ARM: an independent catalogue (CALIFA TW) and the bar-length systematic measured on real galaxies
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  CALIFA TW: 31 galaxies (15 strong bars from Aguerri+2015, 16 weak bars from Cuomo+2019), median R = 0.950, 16-84% [0.62, 1.29]
  CALIFA: 90.3 +- 5.3 per cent have R < 1.4 -- the hunt list's >= 90 per cent criterion is MET here; 52 per cent have R < 1, which is again dynamically impossible
  MaNGA  : 33.3 +- 3.3 per cent (N = 210); CALIFA: 90.3 +- 5.3 per cent (N = 31)
  the two catalogues differ in fast fraction by +57 percentage points, i.e. +9.2 sigma on their own sampling errors alone.  They cannot both be measuring the same population with the same estimator.
  and the same quality trend appears INSIDE CALIFA, independently: Aguerri+2015 print <R> = 1.2 (+0.7/-0.5) for their full 32-galaxy TW compilation and <R> = 1.0 (+0.3/-0.3) for the 23 with uncertainties under 30 per cent -- better measurements again give SMALLER R, the same direction as the MaNGA error-bin table above.
  
  Cuomo+2021 Table A.1 + Table 2: 12 galaxies, each with ONE TW corotation radius divided by up to seven published bar radii.  Within a single galaxy, R spans a factor of 2.12 in the median (16-84%: 1.85 - 2.60, max 3.43).
    WITHIN-galaxy scatter from the bar-length definition alone: 0.140 dex.  BETWEEN-galaxy scatter of the same galaxies' median R: 0.125 dex.  The definitional systematic is 1.1x the real galaxy-to-galaxy signal, so at this precision the bar-length choice, not the galaxy, sets R.
    and for scale: making 90 per cent of the MaNGA sample fast needs the bar radii there to be low by a factor 2.17 -- INSIDE the factor 2.12 that the published definitions already disagree by on the same galaxies.  The systematic that would flip the item is not hypothetical; it is the size of a routine methodological choice.
  [FAIL] 29e SECOND ARM, and it does not rescue the item -- it dissolves it.  An independent Tremaine-Weinberg catalogue (CALIFA) MEETS the hunt list's >= 90 per cent criterion while MaNGA misses it by a factor of three, a difference far larger than either sample's own error, and Cuomo+2021 show that one galaxy's R moves by about a factor of two depending only on which published bar-radius definition is used -- as large as the factor that would flip the MaNGA answer   (CALIFA 90 per cent fast (N = 31) vs MaNGA 33 per cent (N = 210), +9.2 sigma apart; within-galaxy bar-length spread in R = x2.12 (0.140 dex) against a between-galaxy 0.125 dex; the flip factor is x2.17)
  [FAIL] 29f AGAINST INTEREST, and this is the one that stops the CALIFA arm being banked as a win: the catalogue that DOES satisfy the framework's criterion satisfies it partly by putting a large share of its bars at R < 1, i.e. corotation inside the bar, which is not a fast bar but an impossible one.  A framework cannot claim credit for agreement produced by measurements that are themselves unphysical   (CALIFA: 52 per cent of 31 galaxies have R < 1 (MaNGA: 12 per cent); Cuomo+2021 was written specifically to explain those objects and concluded they are a bar-length measurement problem, which is the same systematic that would be needed to move MaNGA's slow tail)
  context [canonical]: the acceleration at corotation is 1.67 a_0 (16-84%: 0.93 - 2.55) -- bars sit right at the transition, but R is dimensionless and no a_0 enters it
  context [alt]: the acceleration at corotation is 1.38 a_0 (16-84%: 0.77 - 2.11) -- bars sit right at the transition, but R is dimensionless and no a_0 enters it
  [FAIL] 29 (NOT DECIDABLE ON THIS CATALOGUE, and reported that way rather than as a kill) at face value the Kepler-grade condition FAILS badly -- only a third of MaNGA bars have R < 1.4 and 79 are individually slow at their published bounds -- but the same catalogue puts its BEST-MEASURED bars at a median R of 0.99 with half of them dynamically impossible.  The answer flips with measurement quality, so the sample cannot settle the item either way   (33.3 +- 3.3 per cent fast (N = 210), median R = 1.663; best-measured 20 bars median 0.99 (50 per cent impossible), worst-measured 37 median 2.71; corr(R, sigma) = +0.64)
  [FAIL] 29b MY OWN DECONVOLUTION FAILED ITS OWN PREDICTIVE CHECK and is withdrawn, not quoted: the maximum-likelihood fit prefers all bars intrinsically fast, but when that best fit is asked to regenerate the data it predicts a large unphysical tail that is not there and a median R far below the observed one   (ML f = 0.00, s = 1.25; predicted unphysical fraction 37.7 +- 3.2 per cent vs observed 11.9 (-8.1 sigma); predicted median 1.19 vs observed 1.66 (+9.4 sigma))
  [PASS] 29c AGAINST INTEREST, the internal inconsistency that dominates everything: 12 per cent of the whole sample, and 50 per cent of the twenty best-measured bars, have corotation INSIDE the bar, which cannot happen.  A catalogue whose cleanest measurements are unphysical cannot be used to count how many bars are slow, and this is stated rather than trimmed away   (11.9 per cent of 210 have R < 1; among the 20 with sigma < 0.2 it is 50 per cent)
  [PASS] 29d AGAINST INTEREST, the item's own limitation, which no amount of extra data removes: R is dimensionless, a_0 never enters and both footings give identical numbers, so this item can never measure a_0 or separate the footings -- it can only test halo dynamical friction, and it does that through a quantity whose value is set by a bar-length convention   (bar radii would need to be low by x2.17 for 90 per cent fast, x1.19 for the median; corotation sits at 1.67 a_0 (canonical) / 1.38 a_0 (alt); every number above is identical on both footings)

